                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I played PataNoir back in 2016 and reviewed it for Just Adventure. Recently, I decided to revisit it and pick up some missing achievements. Once again, I am so impressed by this game. It is not going to appeal to everyone.  For text adventurers who love language, it is quite brilliant. \n\nThe game makes extensive use of the simile (\"It's raining like a cow pissing on a flat rock\") and the metaphor (\"you are the sunshine of my life\"). PataNoir takes its name from the pataphor which extends the metaphor concept to create a new reality. In PataNoir, the previously referenced cow, flat rock, and sunshine become objects that can be interacted with.\n\nThe game has all the hallmarks of the classic noir style: A detective past his prime, a smoky office filled with case files, a client who knocks on the door, and (of course) a beautiful woman. To solve the case, you must travel through multiple locations and interact with interesting characters, using only typed text commands. \n\nIllustrations are provided for each scene and the original sound track is very nice. There is a manual save system with multiple slots that comes in handy if you are an achievement hunter.\n\nThe game starts with a tutorial that is quite helpful. But, as with most text adventures, PataNoir will require that you do battle with the parser to learn its limitations. The complexity is increased as you manage literal and figurative objects. For example: A literal knife found in a drawer can be used to hurt someone. A figurative knife (\"the coin had edges sharp as a knife\") can be used to clear smoke that is \"so thick you can cut it with a knife.\"  Yep... pretty cerebral stuff!\n\nPataNoir is definitely a niche game. If you don't mind typing and visualizing AND if you love to play with words, then you should not miss this experience. Although the story is somewhat linear, it has multiple endings and leaves quite a bit of room for playing with language and watching for what happens next.",
